To: taya@NetBSD.org
Cc: pkgsrc-users@NetBSD.org
Subject: [PATCH] inputmethod/anthy-elisp: accept emacs26 through emacs30, and fix five removed Emacs names

inputmethod/anthy-elisp only accepts emacs21, emacs20 and XEmacs:

  EMACS_VERSIONS_ACCEPTED= emacs21 emacs21nox emacs20 \
                           xemacs215 xemacs215nox xemacs214 xemacs214nox

so on a machine with a current Emacs it stops in can-be-built-here.mk:

  ERROR: Accepted versions are: emacs21 emacs21nox emacs20 xemacs215 ...
  ERROR: No valid Emacs version installed found

That line was added in rev 1.14 (2024-05-08, wiz), whose commit message says
"Since I don't know better, restrict it to the versions that were available
at the time of the packaged version of this software (2009)."  Before 1.14
there was no restriction at all.  So it was a guess made without a test, and
this is the test.

It matters because anthy-elisp is the only package that installs anthy.el
(apart from the tamago ones).  An init file that does

  (load-library "anthy")
  (setq default-input-method 'japanese-anthy)

has no other way to work through pkgsrc.

Widening the list is not enough on its own.  anthy.el and anthy-dic.el use
five names Emacs has since removed.  Only set-face-underline-p (anthy.el:74,
removed in Emacs 29) stops the build -- it is at top level, so anthy-isearch.el
and anthy-kyuri.el fail to byte-compile and their .elc files go missing from
the PLIST.

The other four are inside functions, so byte compilation only warns and the
package installs looking healthy:

  anthy.el:748         process-kill-without-query   removed in Emacs 27
  anthy.el:877         inactivate-input-method      removed in Emacs 29
  anthy.el:895         last-command-char            removed in Emacs 24
  anthy-dic.el:73,116  string-to-int                removed in Emacs 26

process-kill-without-query is the one that matters, and it is the earliest.
It sits in anthy-check-agent, which anthy-do-send-recv-command calls to start
anthy-agent, so from Emacs 27 the package builds cleanly and then dies on the
first conversion request.  That makes Emacs 26 the last version on which
anthy-elisp is usable, not 28 as the build failure alone would suggest.  On
27 and 28 nothing looks wrong until the user types.

The diff below adds emacs26 through emacs30, guards the four calls in
anthy.el with fboundp or boundp, and renames string-to-int in anthy-dic.el.
Guards rather than renames, since emacs20, emacs21 and XEmacs stay in the
accepted list and do not have the new names; string-to-number is the
exception, being available since Emacs 20.

anthy itself has not been released since 2009, so there is nowhere to send
these upstream.  Three other projects got here first, though.

OpenBSD ports patches the same two files with the same substitutions, and
their log shows the same boundary from the other side:

  2020-08-24  jca      Unbreak anthy.el with emacs-27.1
  2020-11-09  yasuoka  Fix anthy.el not to use process-kill-without-query
                       which is deleted.

Their patch cannot be copied here as it stands, though.  They rename where
this one guards, and applying theirs to the same tree gives 4/6 and a build
failure on emacs20 (set-face-underline does not exist yet) and, on emacs21,
a package that builds and then dies in anthy-check-agent with
(void-function set-process-query-on-exit-flag) -- the mirror image of what
unpatched anthy does on Emacs 27, in the same function.  OpenBSD ships one
Emacs; pkgsrc keeps emacs20, emacs21 and XEmacs in the accepted list.

anthy-unicode, the maintained fork, has made the same changes in its own
copy.  FreeBSD and Debian dropped 9100h instead -- FreeBSD ports has only
japanese/anthy-unicode now, and Debian's anthy went from 9100h-24 to 1:0.4-3
with an epoch to mark the switch.

That leaves pkgsrc and Gentoo shipping 9100h's anthy.el unpatched.  So the
choice is between carrying the patch as OpenBSD does, or following FreeBSD
and Debian to anthy-unicode.  If you would rather do the latter, that is a
fair answer to this too.

The patches go in inputmethod/anthy/patches, since anthy-elisp shares
PATCHDIR and DISTINFO_FILE with inputmethod/anthy.  They sort after your
patch-anthy.el and touch different parts of the file -- the old backquote
syntax it fixes is not touched here.  inputmethod/anthy installs no .el files
of its own, so its PKGREVISION is left alone; anthy-elisp's goes to 8.

Built on NetBSD 9.4, 10.1 and 11.0 (i386, under qemu) against pkgsrc trunk
fa7ad771c96929bf742033e6ccfd8bb45b9cab49 with EMACS_TYPE=emacs30nox.
anthy-elisp-9100hnb8 builds on all three with all six .elc files present.

Since the point of the patch is that byte compilation does not catch the
problem, building was not treated as enough.  Every Emacs in
EMACS_VERSIONS_ACCEPTED that pkgsrc packages was run against both trees on
NetBSD 9.4/i386, from the official binary packages.  Each run byte-compiles
the six .el files, then starts emacs, types "nihongo" through anthy-insert,
converts, commits, writes the buffer out and greps it for 日本語.

                    without the patch        with the patch
  emacs20  20.7     6/6  converts           6/6  converts
  emacs21  21.4     6/6  converts           6/6  converts
  emacs26  26.3     6/6  last-command-char  6/6  converts
  emacs27  27.2     6/6  last-command-char  6/6  converts
  emacs28  28.2     6/6  last-command-char  6/6  converts
  emacs29  29.4     4/6  set-face-underline-p   6/6  converts
  emacs30  30.2     4/6  set-face-underline-p   6/6  converts
  xemacs214 21.4.25 6/6  (not exercised)    6/6  (not exercised)

emacs20 and emacs21 still convert with the patch applied, which is what the
guards are for -- widening the list should not cost the versions that already
worked.  The XEmacs row is byte compilation only, since anthy.el takes a
different branch there and reads last-command-event as an event object; the
point of that row is that the patch leaves XEmacs where it found it.

Note also that without the patch anthy is unusable from emacs26 onwards even
where it builds, and only appears to work because init files carry
(define-obsolete-variable-alias 'last-command-char 'last-command-event ...)
to paper over it.

I have also sent this as a PR to gnats-bugs@ so it does not get lost.

Thanks,

--
